      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Human airway smooth muscle cells (HASMs) are involved in the pathogenesis of asthma. By producing chemokines, HASMs play a role in the inflammatory processes observed in this disease. Eotaxin, eotaxin-2, and eotaxin-3 are important chemoattractants for eosinophils, and these chemokines are expressed during different phases of the allergic reaction. TH2 cytokines and TGF-beta can be found in increased levels in patients with asthma, and these cytokines may be involved in the regulation of chemokine expression."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to determine the effect of TH2 cytokines and TGF-beta on the regulation of expression of eotaxin, eotaxin-2, and eotaxin-3 by HASMs."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "HASMs were incubated for 24 hours with IL-4, IL-13, TGF-beta1, or combinations of these cytokines. Protein and mRNA levels of eotaxin and eotaxin-3 were evaluated by sandwich ELISA and reverse transcriptase-PCR."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "IL-4 and IL-13 induced mRNA and protein for both eotaxin and eotaxin-3. Eotaxin-2 mRNA and protein were not detected in HASMs. TGF-beta alone did not induce expression of the eotaxins. However, in combination with IL-4 or IL-13, TGF-beta enhanced eotaxin production and inhibited TH2 cytokine-induced eotaxin-3 production."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "TGF-beta differentially regulates TH2 cytokine-induced eotaxin and eotaxin-3 release."
        }
      ]},
